    {
      "id": "evidence-000-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "The grep pins the WHOLE rendered type, not the name, and that is the point of this row rather than tidiness. `build_nat_prelude` admits the declaration through the trusted `Kernel::add_declaration` gate, which re-checks the proof term against the stated type -- so the kernel already refuses a false theorem, and a witness that was not prime (say `m` itself at `m = 6`) never gets in. What the kernel cannot notice is a statement that is WEAKER than intended. Measured 2026-08-17: spelling the primality lower bound `1 <= p` instead of `2 <= p` still type-checks, still admits, still passes every existing test including axiom-freedom and the determinism count -- and is satisfied by `p = 1`, whose only divisor is 1, which closes nothing. That mutation was run: exactly one test died (`every_number_at_least_two_has_a_prime_divisor`, which compares the admitted type against an independently built term) and 288 others passed. A name-only grep would have survived it, so this command greps the `AxNat.succ (AxNat.succ AxNat.zero)` bound inside the existential explicitly. `nat_theorem_inventory` additionally exits non-zero for a name that does not exist.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
      "provenance": {
        "command": "python3 render/producers-py/facts_to_docir.py",
        "epoch": {
          "commit": "d637d83f77dbfa43c98eee4ab0ad78d235099006",
